> > Izchak Miller himself coded shopkeepers to be killable. I don't get it
> > why people keep making such a fuss about killing the light shopkeeper.

> Izchak Miller, one of the founding members of the DevTeam, passed away
> on April 1, 1994 from complications due to cancer. As he was
> responsible for, among other things, much of the shopkeeper logic, the
> owner of the lighting shop was named for him as a tribute. The DevTeam
> dedicated version 3.2 of NetHack to the memory of Izchak Miller. Many
> players who will happily slay everything else that moves will spare
> Izchak's life as a sign of respect.
>
> For a little more info, including a post from Izchak's
> daughter, check out the following thread from 1996 in rgrn:
> <http://tinyurl.com/r7l23>

Ill-posed questions get bad responses, my fault. The above is a good
answer to "why would those who worked personally with Izchak Miller
commit some gesture of commemoration", but it is very lazy thinking
for "why should I not kill the light store shopkeeper."  From the
minute on they are dead, people get disproportionate amounts of
respect and reveree for things that were entirely mundane before -
which is quite hypocritical.

So, yes, Izchak Miller deserved a lot of credit and admiration for his
work on NetHack. But, no, you may not compell me to treat the
shopkeeper with the same respect you show the gravestones of your
family.


